        videoUrl: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZHCB09O6zUk",
        content:
          "Last lesson defined AI. This lesson answers two questions people often ask: if AI is so new, why do we hear that it started in the 1950s? And what really makes AI software different from the programs we have used for decades?\n\n## A Short History in Six Steps\n\n| Period | What happened |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1950 | Mathematician Alan Turing asks \"Can machines think?\" and proposes a test: if you cannot tell a machine's typed answers from a person's, does it matter? |\n| 1956 | The term \"artificial intelligence\" is used at a summer workshop at Dartmouth College in the United States. The field is born. |\n| 1960s to 1980s | Researchers build **rule-based systems** (\"expert systems\") that follow thousands of hand-written rules. They work in narrow areas but are slow to build and break easily. Funding dries up twice in periods called **AI winters**. |\n| 1997 | A computer called Deep Blue beats the world chess champion, Garry Kasparov. |\n| 2010s | Three things arrive together: cheap computing power, huge amounts of digital data, and better learning methods. **Machine learning** and **deep learning** take off: face unlock, voice assistants, much better translation. |\n| 2022 onward | **Generative AI** chat tools reach hundreds of millions of everyday users, writing text, code and images on request. |\n\nThe big lesson from history: AI did not suddenly appear. Ideas from the 1950s only became practical once computers were fast enough and there was enough data to learn from.\n\n## Two Ways to Make a Computer Do Something\n\nImagine you want a computer to sort incoming emails into \"spam\" and \"not spam\".\n\n**The traditional way** is to write rules: \"If the email contains the words *free money*, mark it as spam. If it comes from a known contact, mark it as safe.\" This works until spammers change their wording. Then a programmer must write new rules, again and again.\n\n**The AI (machine learning) way** is to give the computer thousands of example emails that people have already marked as spam or not spam. The computer finds the patterns itself: certain words, certain senders, certain link styles. When spammers change tactics, you retrain the model on fresh examples instead of rewriting every rule.\n\n## The Difference at a Glance\n\n| | Traditional software | AI software |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| How it works | Follows rules a programmer wrote | Learns patterns from examples (data) |\n| Example | A payroll system calculating tax | A system flagging suspicious payments |\n| When the situation changes | A programmer rewrites the rules | The model is retrained on new data |\n| Its mistakes | Predictable, usually caused by bugs | Less predictable, often caused by poor or biased data |\n| Can it explain itself? | Yes, you can read the rules | Often only partly |\n\nNeither is \"better\". A payroll calculation must follow the law exactly every time, so fixed rules are right. Recognising a face in thousands of lighting conditions is impossible to write as rules, so learning from examples is right. Good AI professionals know when **not** to use AI.\n\n## Key Words So Far\n\n- **Algorithm:** a set of step-by-step instructions, like a recipe.\n- **Data:** facts and examples (numbers, words, pictures, sounds) a computer can use.\n- **Model:** what an AI system builds after learning from data. The model is what makes predictions.\n- **Rule-based system:** software that follows hand-written rules, with no learning.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nAI started as an idea in the 1950s, struggled through rule-based systems and AI winters, and became practical in the 2010s when data and computing power caught up. The key difference from traditional software is simple: traditional software follows rules people write, while AI learns patterns from examples. Tomorrow you will see exactly how that learning works.",
