        durationMinutes: 25,
        videoUrl: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5HQCNAsSO-s",
        content:
          "Businesses receive huge amounts of text: reviews, emails, chat messages, survey comments and social media posts. Nobody can read it all. NLP lets an organisation understand thousands of messages in minutes.\n\n## Sentiment Analysis\n\n**Sentiment analysis** works out the emotional tone of text: positive, negative or neutral, sometimes with a score.\n\nThink of a shop manager reading the faces of customers walking out: smiling, neutral or frowning. Sentiment analysis does the same for written feedback, at scale.\n\nUsing a pre-trained model:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import pipeline\nsentiment = pipeline(\"sentiment-analysis\")\n\nreviews = [\n    \"The staff were friendly and the delivery was fast.\",\n    \"Third time my order has arrived damaged. Very disappointed.\",\n    \"It was okay.\",\n]\nfor r in reviews:\n    print(sentiment(r), r)\n```\n\nTypical output: `[{'label': 'POSITIVE', 'score': 0.99}]`.\n\n## Using Sentiment in Business\n\n- Track sentiment week by week to spot a problem early.\n- Compare sentiment by product, branch or region.\n- Route very negative messages to a senior staff member quickly.\n- Combine with topic classification: \"negative\" plus \"delivery\" points to a specific fix.\n\n**Watch out for:** sarcasm (\"Great, another late delivery\"), mixed reviews (\"lovely product, terrible service\"), and local slang or mixed languages that a model trained mostly on formal text may misread. Always check a sample by hand.\n\n## Named Entity Recognition (NER)\n\n**Named entity recognition** finds and labels important items in text:\n\n> \"Hi, I ordered two **solar lanterns** (**PRODUCT**) on **12 March** (**DATE**) with order number **58213** (**NUMBER**), and **Grace** (**PERSON**) from your **Riverside** (**LOCATION**) branch said it would arrive in a week.\"\n\n```python\nner = pipeline(\"ner\", aggregation_strategy=\"simple\")\nprint(ner(\"Grace from the Riverside branch called on 12 March.\"))\n```\n\nNER is used to pull details out of emails, contracts, invoices and reports automatically, which is exactly the \"extract\" step from Day 11's workflow.\n\n## Text Classification for Real Work\n\nYou can classify text in three ways:\n\n1. **Train your own classifier** on labelled examples (bag of words or embeddings plus a model from Day 6).\n2. **Zero-shot classification:** give a pre-trained model the category names and let it decide, with no training.\n3. **Prompt an LLM** with few-shot examples (Day 10).\n\n```python\nzero_shot = pipeline(\"zero-shot-classification\")\nprint(zero_shot(\n    \"My card was charged twice for one order\",\n    candidate_labels=[\"billing\", \"delivery\", \"product quality\", \"account access\"],\n))\n```\n\n## Chatbots: From Menus to Conversations\n\nThere are three broad kinds of chatbot:\n\n| Type | How it works | Strengths and weaknesses |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rule-based (menu) | Fixed buttons and keyword rules: \"Press 1 for billing\" | Predictable, but frustrating when your question does not fit |\n| Intent-based | Classifies what the user wants (intent) and extracts details (entities), then follows a script | Good for common tasks like \"track my order\" |\n| LLM-based | A large language model writes replies, often using RAG on company documents | Natural and flexible, but must be controlled to avoid wrong answers |\n\n## Designing a Good Customer-Service Chatbot\n\n1. **Define its scope:** which questions should it answer? List the top 20 real customer questions.\n2. **Ground it in facts:** connect it to approved documents (RAG) so it answers from policy, not imagination.\n3. **Set clear rules:** what it must never do, such as promise refunds, give legal or medical advice, or ask for passwords.\n4. **Hand over to a human:** when the user is upset, the question is outside scope or the bot is unsure.\n5. **Be honest:** tell users they are talking to an automated assistant.\n6. **Review conversations regularly** to find failures and new questions.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nSentiment analysis measures tone at scale, named entity recognition extracts key details, and text classification sorts messages, using your own model, zero-shot models or prompted LLMs. Chatbots range from simple menus to LLM assistants; the best are grounded in approved information, honest about being automated and quick to hand over to a person. Tomorrow you move from language to images: how computers see.",
